Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.030 align:start position:0%
 
I'm joined today by Gordon Whitman who's  deputy director of faith in action he's  also author of the book stand up how to  get involved speak out and win in a  world on fire this is a very interesting  issue to me Gordon because we've seen  particularly in light of the current  administration a lot of new interest and  new voices sort of enter the political  discussion but with a pretty significant  amount of frustration in many cases that  they're sort of unable to really affect  change or be activists in a way that  will influence the world around them so  the idea of exploring the best ways for  people to be engaged and to impact the  world around them is a really important  one what do you see as sort of the  primary limitations that might lead to  some people feeling as though their  voices are not really relevant yeah it's  interesting David I think we don't  always have enough organizations to  contain the energy that exists and then  sometimes those organizations aren't are  build and built and organized well  enough to really take that that desire  that people have to make change and give  them an outlet for it so it has been a  remarkable year the number of people  who've become involved in politics  I talked to a woman in Annapolis  Maryland who told me she was spending 70  hours a week  sharing her or leading her individual  chapter and first time involved in  politics or grassroots activity in her  life and there's thousands of people in  that position and and I think you know  it's been a remarkable year but we need  more organizations and they need to be  better organized and that's really what  I try to capture in and stand up and  I've worked as a community organizer for  about 25 years so really trying to  capture that that learning about you  know how do you make meetings work  better and be more useful for people's  time and how do you think about how  someone becomes a really a leader of  change in their own community that's  interesting because when one says as you  do that at least part of the issue is  lack of organization that in a way  suggests that it's actually not a  systemic problem that our political  system is conceivably set up in a way  that all of these newfound activists  sort of that have been created over the  last fourteen months or so that they  actually could be having a greater  impact if only they organized themselves  better what about the other side of it  which is sort of the way that the system  is designed to maintain the status quo  in the structures of power well yeah  David I think that's correct that you  know we have a constitution that you  know we're taught to revere but it was  created to make change extraordinarily  difficult and it's no accident that it  was written mostly by men who owned  slaves and wanted to maintain a status  quo and we can see the levels of  frustration that people feel you know  throughout history and certainly now but  why is it so hard to get Congress to act  on things that the American people  support and I think it's built into our  structure the size of our country the  diversity that changed there are a lot  of obstacles to change given that you  know what usually works and it takes  time is for people to organize  themselves effectively and I do think  it's the thing we have the most control  over to build better organizations you  know not just talk to people are  involved but be willing to go out knock  on people's doors reach people aren't  already part of some kind of social  change effort and and invite them in and  I don't think we're doing enough of that  to take one issue that you would you  would have thought at the time at least  would have been sort of a simple  political issue to solve which was in  the wake of the Sandy Hook Matthew in  Connecticut there was somewhere between  85 and 90 percent support for what we  would call universal background checks  for any gun sale whether it's at a gun  show between two people any situation a  background check must be done  overwhelming support from Democrats and  even from a majority of Republicans on  that issue didn't get it done just did  you know and there's a couple there's a  couple things people point to one would  be well yes but the gun lobby has such  financial influence that it was an issue  of the the status quo as we talked about  others say no those people needed to  better  organized in order to get that done how  do you assess that particular one yeah I  think that both things are true that you  know if if if Congress and any  legislature at state level was really  paying attention to what people want  we'd have a very different country so  you can go across the board whether it's  gun gun violence prevention and assault  weapon regulation to paid sick time to  minimum wage to you know so many issues  where the public supports a set of  policies and then we watch over and over  that legislators do the opposite and  that's you know the influence of money  is is tremendous and it's driving a lot  of what happens in Washington and our  state capitals but I think the other  part is it is possible to overcome that  and that's part of what the book is  about is you know it would be nice or  wasn't so but but that's just you know  part of how this democracy works that it  depends on us and citizens how well we  organize and it is possible to build  organizing campaigns that change public  policy and you know we're watching right  now this whole controversy about the  shutdown what's going to happen to  dreamers that program daca that provides  legal status ability to go to school and  work and get driver's licenses for  immigrant youth that was really an  organizing campaign that at the time  when immigrant youth stood up and said  we're here we're not going anywhere  we're not afraid and we need protection  for ourselves and our parents and our  families you know people said well you  know you can't do that and and we can't  do this you know we can't do dakka dakka  was organizing campaign the young people  fought for and won and now it's as the  center of our politics so you look at  the accomplishments whether it's minimum  wage or um earned sick time or  protections food food safety  environmental protection those were  things that we achieved because people  were willing to get involved and often  they started in their local communities  and I think that's a piece we often miss  if we get caught up in what's happening  in Washington what's happening with the  president but a lot of the change we  need to see happen he's going to begin  by making our cities and our states exam  apples and then building pressure or  federal change in terms of tactics right  what do you believe has been shown to be  the most or some of the most effective  tactics when we are presented with you  know writing letters to elected  officials boycotts of different kinds  demonstrations rallies marches getting  involved in politics oneself whatever  write the full possible list what what  is your sense of the most effective  tactics for creating change yes so the  evidence says its face to face would  people have a direct stake making the  case now that that's not the only thing  that works but I tell story in the book  about uh about a group of people who  came back from being in the prison  system in California to living in  Richmond California we're trying to put  their lives back together and faced a  lot of obstacles to finding work and  housing and began to organize in their  in Richmond California to make it easier  for people to find work and put their  you know get their lives back on track  and ultimately became involved in an  effort to reduce violence in the city  and then made the case that the city  didn't need a bigger jail it needed drug  treatment and job opportunities for  people who were in the criminal justice  system so they accomplished a lot in  their City and really made their City a  model getting the city to decide not to  build a bigger jail but to put that  money into education and drug treatment  instead and then that example really  became a major part of a statewide  campaign to move resources from the jail  system to the education system in the  state and I think that's how it you know  those were a lot of face to face  organizing where people who knew the  issue when it met with decision makers  when necessary to held rallies maybe  they engaged in civil disobedience so  that you can use a lot of tactics but it  begins when people have a most at stake  really standing up and saying you know  change is possible this is what we need  we we know we know this you know we know  this system we've had experience and and  meet directly with the  Maker's is there any value either  directly or by virtue of how it might I  guess engage new people of signing  online petitions sharing online  petitions you know there's some online  campaigns that have been very successful  groups like Color of Change have really  impacted corporate policies through  online campaigns so they have a role to  play but if you look at both what  happens today and in the past usually  it's groups and engage people  face-to-face but we have a lot of the  organization I work for it's a national  network Nathan Action used to be called  Pico we have a lot of research on what  helps people get involved and stay  involved and take greater leadership  it's almost always the face-to-face  opportunity to be with another person  and tell your story and build a close  trusted relationship with other people  in your community who share common  interests and then be able to go meet  with and directly confront people in  positions of power that are making  decisions so you know rallies online  petitions there are all tactics and have  a role to play but they need to be based  on organizations that people are  actually members of and we've lost touch  with that membership you know now you're  a member of an organization because they  have your email address and they send  you emails or you sign an online  petition but you're not really a member  of an organization unless you're meeting  with other people who are also members  and that social media doesn't does it do  away with the necessity for that kind of  grassroots face-to-face community civic  engagement Gordon do you share my  frustration and I know that it's not the  most useful frustration in terms of  being forward-looking but the  frustration I have that if only a  fraction of these newly engaged  individuals had just voted in November  of 2016 that a lot of this just wouldn't  happen when it came down to  seventy-seven thousand votes in three  states that are not historically very  red states that does do you share that  frustration and does that frustration  have some kind of place in figuring out  our future plans yeah I think we should  be frustrated that everyone's not voting  and and it would we  live in a very different country I think  we won we need to be clear that he's a  very intentional effort over the last  decade to make it much more difficult  for people to vote yeah  and that's been systematic it's been  focused primarily on on black and Latino  voters and it's been just very  pernicious from closing voting sites in  certain communities to requiring IDs and  then making it difficult to obtain them  you know we're just seeing a very  systematic effort to make it harder for  people to vote and and that doesn't  affect everyone but if it affects you  know 1% or 2% of voters who maybe don't  drive so now I'm driver's licenses you  can affect the outcome of an election  which is why that's been such a big  focus of efforts to try to hold on to  power particularly among conservatives  so I think you know let's pay attention  to that and then you know people vote in  part because the election is relevant  for them and and I think we fail when we  don't make the election relevant when we  don't have issues on the ballot when we  don't have candidates that are really  speaking to what people care most about  and then it's just hard work to motivate  people to vote and you know primarily  people vote because other people they  know are voting so my organization in  2016 through our local affiliates had  800,000 conversations with voters who  don't usually vote and what we find is  that if you have those conversations you  engage people at their doors and their  congregations in their communities  people are much more likely to vote but  we need to do more of that and you know  that's the big question is is this  August resistance and protest marches do  they translate into political power and  that that requires creating organization  and that's really our challenge right  now it's to build organizations that  people can be part of can lead and use  to govern their communities the book is  stand up how to get involved speak out  and win in a world on fire we've been  speaking with the book's author Gordon  Whitman really appreciate you talking to  me about this Gordon Thanks

